\section{Value scale}{

BMR and BMC use the plate-normalized value \code{endpoint_value_norm} of
\code{\link[=generate_behavior_endpoints]{generate_behavior_endpoints()}}; the functions have no default for
\code{value_col}, so the scale is always a deliberate choice. Statistics
(Module 4) use \code{endpoint_value_raw} instead: ANOVA compares doses within
one plate, whereas a BMR is shared across plates and chemicals and needs
the normalized scale. For Distance Change/Shift the two columns are equal
(no normalization); for Movement Similarity and Total Distance they are
not.
}

\section{Data prepared by the caller}{

Curvep takes treated concentrations only, on the log10 molar scale:
remove vehicle-control rows and transform the concentration (e.g.
\code{log10(concentration / 1e6)} for micromolar) before calling
\code{\link[=estimate_bmr_curvep]{estimate_bmr_curvep()}} or \code{\link[=run_bmc_curvep]{run_bmc_curvep()}}. The \code{chemical} column is
one dose-response unit: chemical x plate for per-plate curves, or the
chemical when plates are pooled.
}

\section{Monte Carlo and run time}{

Curvep bootstraps each curve (\code{n_samples}, default 1000), so
\code{hit_confidence} and the BMC confidence interval vary slightly with the
seed and the R version; set \code{seed} for reproducible runs. A full batch
can take a long time. Rcurvep respects the active \code{future} plan, so run
\code{future::plan(future::multisession, workers = n)} before
\code{\link[=run_bmc_curvep]{run_bmc_curvep()}} to use several cores; the package never sets a plan.
}

